From 94779181b31284c6f8fa0841df43b1fd6cc89545 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:03:30 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] kernel-settings: zram by hardware, through zram-generator's
 own directive

Daniel's design: zram off on capable machines, on where it pays. zram-auto
answers 1 or 0 for zram-generator's set! directive and the zram size is
multiplied by it, so a capable machine gets no zram unit at all, even with
Debian's default [zram0] present. Automatic: on with 8 GB of RAM or less,
or a swap area on eMMC, an SD card, a rotating disk or removable storage
(through LUKS or LVM, the disks underneath decide). ZRAM=auto|on|off in
/etc/default/sparky-stereo-zram overrides it. lz4: on real desktop memory
zstd stored a third less but cost about twice the CPU to compress and to
read back. Swappiness stays 1; in a 4 GB VM zram took 2.1 GiB of pressure
with no OOM kill, and the same run with zram off was OOM-killed.
README: a user's own sysctl file must sort after 70-sparky-stereo.conf
(measured: 00-mine.conf loses, 99-mine.conf wins).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus via Claude Code
